    {
      "name": "In what order",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "answer",
          "question": "Should one algebra of changes come before annotated relations?",
          "answer": [
            "string/concat",
            [
              "cond",
              [
                [
                  "and",
                  [
                    "stated",
                    {
                      "covers": "annotated relations",
                      "by": "dbsp"
                    },
                    true
                  ],
                  [
                    "stated",
                    {
                      "covers": "annotated relations",
                      "by": "semiring provenance"
                    },
                    true
                  ]
                ],
                "One algebra of changes is annotated relations whose annotations are integers, so the two are one design and not two. "
              ],
              ["else", ""]
            ],
            [
              "cond",
              [
                [
                  "and",
                  ["handles", "incremental", "semi-naive evaluation", "?_"],
                  ["handles", "incremental", "re-deriving and comparing", "?_"]
                ],
                "Its one rule would take the place of both Lion's semi-naive evaluation and its fall-back of deriving a relation again and comparing, which are separate code today. "
              ],
              ["else", ""]
            ],
            [
              "cond",
              [
                ["violated", "li-order", "?_"],
                "But that rule needs annotations that can be subtracted, so building it first with integers alone would fix a choice the annotated relations need to make. "
              ],
              [
                [
                  "stated",
                  {
                    "covers": "annotations without subtraction",
                    "by": "candidate multisets"
                  },
                  true
                ],
                "Its rule needs values that can be subtracted, but annotations need not be: kept as columns that aggregates combine, only their candidates are ever added and removed, so integer weights are enough and annotated relations need no rework of it. "
              ],
              ["else", ""]
            ],
            [
              "cond",
              [
                [
                  "stated",
                  {
                    "covers": "min without the full set",
                    "by": "dbsp"
                  },
                  false
                ],
                "Min and max cannot be subtracted: to survive deletions they must keep every candidate."
              ],
              ["else", ""]
            ]
          ],
          "evidence": [
            "query",
            [
              "and",
              [
                "in",
                "?f",
                [
                  "array/make",
                  "annotated relations",
                  "semi-naive evaluation",
                  "re-deriving and comparing",
                  "annotations without subtraction",
                  "min without the full set"
                ]
              ],
              [
                "answer",
                {
                  "covers": "?f",
                  "by": "?technique"
                },
                "?covers",
                "?statement"
              ]
            ],
            {
              "order": ["f", "technique"]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
